---
import Prose from '@/layouts/Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from '@/components/site/CodeBlock.astro';
import DevNav from '@/components/site/DevNav.astro';
import Installer from '@/components/site/Installer.svelte';
import { CLAUDE_PLUGIN, MCP_INSTALL, MCP_INSTALL_LINKS, MCP_TOKENS, installerCatalog } from '@/developers';
import { formatNumber } from '@/developers/installer';

const config = `npx roundertools config                  # the folder, the settings and your favourites
npx roundertools config set tools all    # lean, all, default, families or tool names
npx roundertools config set memory off   # stop counting which tools you use
npx roundertools config reset            # forget the settings and the counts`;
const usage = `{ "version": 1, "tools": { "compress-pdf": { "count": 4, "last": "2026-11-02" } } }`;
---

<Prose
  title="Rounder MCP server: local PDF, image and video tools for AI assistants"
  description="@roundertools/mcp gives Claude, Cursor and VS Code Rounder's file tools. It runs on your computer over stdio, reads only the folders you allow, and never uploads a file."
  heading="The <em>MCP</em> server"
>
  <DevNav current="/developers/mcp" />
  <p>
    <code>@roundertools/mcp</code> is a <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io">Model Context Protocol</a> server that lets an
    AI assistant use Rounder's tools on files on your computer: "compress the PDFs in my Downloads folder under 2 MB", "make
    these screenshots into one PDF", "cut this video to the first 30 seconds". It runs locally over stdio, so your files
    stay where they are; the assistant only sees the results' paths, sizes and summaries.
  </p>

  <Installer client:idle catalog={installerCatalog} />

  <h2 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={MCP_INSTALL.claudeCode} />
  <p>
    <code>--scope user</code> makes it available in every project. <code>--allow</code> names a folder it may read;
    <code>~</code> works in every config below, JSON included. Repeat it for more folders:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={MCP_INSTALL.claudeCodeAllow} />
  <p>
    Or skip the server. The <code>roundertools</code> plugin is a skill that teaches Claude Code to run the
    <a href="/developers/cli">CLI</a> in its terminal, so you approve each command like any other. Until it's needed it
    costs about 100 tokens. From inside Claude Code:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="claude code" code={CLAUDE_PLUGIN} />

  <h2>Claude Desktop</h2>
  <p>Settings → Developer → Edit config, then add it to <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock label="claude_desktop_config.json" code={MCP_INSTALL.claudeDesktop} />
  <p>The same works on Windows, where <code>~</code> is your user folder too.</p>

  <h2>Cursor</h2>
  <p><a href={MCP_INSTALL_LINKS.cursor}>Add to Cursor</a> in one click, or put it in <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> (every project) or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> (one project):</p>
  <CodeBlock label="mcp.json" code={MCP_INSTALL.cursor} />

  <h2>VS Code</h2>
  <p><a href={MCP_INSTALL_LINKS.vscode}>Add to VS Code</a> in one click, or put it in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock label=".vscode/mcp.json" code={MCP_INSTALL.vscode} />

  <h2>Which files it can touch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reading:</strong> the folders you pass with <code>--allow</code> (repeat it for more), or in <code>ROUNDERTOOLS_ALLOW</code>. Without either, your home folder and the folder it was started in. A client's roots can narrow that further, never widen it.</li>
    <li><strong>Never:</strong> hidden folders and files (<code>.ssh</code>, <code>.aws</code>, <code>.env</code>…), key and password files, and app data such as browser profiles, even inside an allowed folder. Symlinks are followed to where they really point before checking.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing:</strong> only next to the input, or into a folder you name with <code>--out</code>. It never replaces an existing file and never touches an input. Its own <a href="#settings">settings folder</a> is the one other place it writes.</li>
    <li><strong>Network:</strong> none. It has no code that makes a connection, and it blocks outgoing connections and DNS lookups in its own process as well. That guard isn't a sandbox: video and audio run on your own ffmpeg, a separate program, which it starts limited to local files.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Text and details it reads out of a file (PDF to text, a PDF's title, EXIF fields) are marked as the file's content, not instructions, because a document can
    contain words written to mislead an assistant.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets loaded</h2>
  <p>
    An assistant reads the description of every tool a server lists at the start of every conversation, whether it uses
    them or not. So by default the server lists only three, plus a short paragraph of instructions: about {formatNumber(MCP_TOKENS.lean)} tokens.
    <code>find-tool</code> searches every tool on the site by what you want done, <code>describe-tool</code> shows a tool's
    options, and <code>run-tool</code> runs any of them that work outside a browser (<code>find-tool</code> links to the
    site for the rest).
  </p>
  <p>
    Behind them are 37 tools: PDF (compress, merge, split, organize, protect, unlock, page numbers, watermark, to text,
    to JPG, extract images, form fields, fill forms, true redaction), documents (Word, PowerPoint, Excel, CSV or PDF as
    Markdown, a spreadsheet inspector), images (convert, compress, resize, crop, rotate, remove EXIF, prepare for AI, diff),
    ZIP files, file info, and, when ffmpeg is installed, video and audio, including a contact sheet of a video. When the assistant describes one or runs it successfully, the server adds it to
    the list, so next time it calls it by name. Tools you use often are listed from the start (see
    <a href="#memory">memory</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--tools all</code> lists every tool up front instead, about {formatNumber(MCP_TOKENS.all)} tokens. <code>--tools pdf,image</code> lists just
    those families, and <code>--tools default</code> the set 0.1 listed.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also offers prompts, which cost nothing until you pick one: <code>compress-pdf</code>, <code>merge-pdfs</code>,
    <code>convert-images</code>, <code>pdf-to-text</code> and, with ffmpeg, <code>shrink-video</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="memory">Memory</h2>
  <p>
    To know which tools you use, the server counts them. After a tool works it adds one to that tool's count and notes the
    date. Tools used at least twice in the last 90 days, up to five, are listed from the start next time. This is all it
    keeps, in a file on your computer:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="usage.json" code={usage} />
  <p>
    Tool names, counts and dates: never paths, file names, options or anything from a file. Turn it off with
    <code>--no-memory</code>, <code>ROUNDERTOOLS_MEMORY=off</code> or <code>npx roundertools config set memory off</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
  <p>
    The settings and the counts live in <code>%APPDATA%\roundertools</code> on Windows,
    <code>~/Library/Application Support/roundertools</code> on macOS and <code>~/.config/roundertools</code> elsewhere
    (<code>ROUNDERTOOLS_CONFIG_DIR</code> picks another folder). The CLI shows and changes them; the server reads them when
    it starts, and its own flags win.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="terminal" code={config} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--allow DIR</code>: a folder it may read (repeatable)</li>
    <li><code>--out DIR</code>: a folder it may write results to, besides next to the inputs</li>
    <li><code>--tools LIST</code>: <code>lean</code> (the default), <code>all</code>, <code>default</code>, families (<code>pdf,image,files,media</code>) or tool names</li>
    <li><code>--no-memory</code>: don't count which tools you use</li>
    <li><code>--experimental</code>: features that aren't finished (none yet)</li>
    <li><code>--max-input SIZE</code>, <code>--timeout MINUTES</code>, <code>--max-outputs N</code>: limits per call</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It speaks MCP over stdio with the official TypeScript SDK, logs to stderr only, runs one heavy job at a time, reports
    progress and stops when a call is cancelled.
  </p>
</Prose>
